## Outside without leaving

Eighteen square metres with a balcony of your own, which in a city built this tightly counts for a great deal. Two black bistro chairs sit out there facing a broad tree and the brick facades on the far side of the street, and on a warm morning that is where the first coffee of the day is best drunk.

Inside, the two single beds sit against the same panel of sage geometric wallpaper the hotel uses throughout, with the deep red carpet running underneath. Full-height glazing gives on to the balcony and floods the room, so even on a grey Amsterdam afternoon it never feels closed in. A framed print hangs on the wall by the door, and a white desk runs along the opposite side.

The three extra square metres over a standard Twin go into the space around the beds rather than the beds themselves, which is what makes the room easy to share: two cases can be open at once without either of you stepping over the other.

Safe, iron and a full wardrobe are standard here, alongside the seating area, the flat-screen TV and the coffee machine found in every room. The bathroom has a corner shower, a wall-hung basin and a heated towel rail.

The Albert Cuypmarkt is around the corner, the Museumplein five minutes away by tram. Reception can arrange a canal cruise, museum entry or a tram ticket before you go out.
